WEBVTT

1
00:00:02,002 --> 00:00:04,505
[♪ slow, pensive music begins]

2
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:12,471
[Dr. Rose] <i>I wanna ask questions about...</i>

3
00:00:13,972 --> 00:00:17,100
<i>what happened within the family</i>
<i>that brought you to therapy.</i>

4
00:00:17,184 --> 00:00:18,852
[♪ music continues]

5
00:00:24,942 --> 00:00:27,527
[Sophia "Arguelles" Maddox] <i>You know,</i>
<i>I don't know, I come from a, a murderer.</i>

6
00:00:28,654 --> 00:00:30,614
♪♪

7
00:00:32,908 --> 00:00:34,618
<i>You know, who would...</i>

8
00:00:36,286 --> 00:00:38,455
<i>who the fuck would accept me?</i>

9
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:45,754
<i>Uh, so what else do you need to know?</i>

10
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:53,178
<i>Maybe the best way to start this</i>
<i>is to talk about my dad.</i>

11
00:00:56,014 --> 00:00:59,977
<i>My dad... was a great dad.</i>

12
00:01:01,436 --> 00:01:05,274
Are you my babys? Are you my babys? Huh?

13
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:08,443
[Sophia] <i>Growing up, I felt like he and I</i>

14
00:01:08,527 --> 00:01:11,029
<i>were the only two people who existed.</i>

15
00:01:11,113 --> 00:01:13,115
She's flying through the air!

16
00:01:14,199 --> 00:01:16,577
[Sophia] <i>Everyone else</i>
<i>was living in our world.</i>

17
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:19,079
<i>My parents were divorced,</i>

18
00:01:19,162 --> 00:01:21,540
<i>but my dad took care of me part-time.</i>

19
00:01:21,623 --> 00:01:23,500
[Daniel Arguelles]
Time to get dressed for school.

20
00:01:24,585 --> 00:01:27,170
[Sophia] <i>His biggest gift to me</i>
<i>was his imagination.</i>

21
00:01:28,297 --> 00:01:30,591
<i>He was a writer, actor, and director.</i>

22
00:01:30,674 --> 00:01:32,426
What, are you kidding me?
The President's gonna be there.

23
00:01:33,010 --> 00:01:35,554
[Sophia] <i>But he never</i>
<i>quite made it as a filmmaker.</i>

24
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:37,764
<i>Until one day...</i>

25
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:40,601
<i>he did.</i>

26
00:01:40,684 --> 00:01:42,144
[Daniel] Action.

27
00:01:42,227 --> 00:01:43,979
[yelling]

28
00:01:44,563 --> 00:01:47,107
[Sophia]
<i>He filmed every second of my life.</i>

29
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:49,234
♪♪

30
00:01:49,318 --> 00:01:52,237
<i>There was nothing</i>
<i>we wouldn't do for each other.</i>

31
00:01:52,321 --> 00:01:56,408
[young Sophia]
Can I, um, videotape, um, the world?

32
00:01:57,617 --> 00:01:59,369
[Daniel] That's what
you're doing right now.

33
00:02:00,996 --> 00:02:03,206
You're videotaping the world.

34
00:02:04,708 --> 00:02:06,918
[young Sophia] <i>Oh, you're gonna like this.</i>

35
00:02:08,337 --> 00:02:10,297
[traffic rumbling]

36
00:02:12,257 --> 00:02:13,925
[Sophia] <i>I'm now that LA cliché</i>

37
00:02:14,009 --> 00:02:17,846
<i>of a struggling actress,</i>
<i>babysitter, wannabe filmmaker.</i>

38
00:02:20,932 --> 00:02:24,853
<i>I work a bunch of jobs,</i>
<i>and I have 50 bucks in the bank.</i>

39
00:02:30,734 --> 00:02:34,738
<i>And then, my dad and I just, uh,</i>
<i>got some really bad news.</i>

40
00:02:34,821 --> 00:02:36,823
[♪ somber music playing]

41
00:02:39,201 --> 00:02:40,827
<i>It shattered our worlds.</i>

42
00:02:42,871 --> 00:02:45,666
[crew talking indistinctly]

43
00:02:45,749 --> 00:02:46,833
-[crew member] Bobby, you good?
-[Bobby] Yeah.

44
00:02:46,917 --> 00:02:47,918
[crew member] Okay, cool.

45
00:02:48,001 --> 00:02:50,045
This is Daniel's interview.

46
00:02:50,128 --> 00:02:51,963
[Sophia] <i>I'm terrified.</i>

47
00:02:54,091 --> 00:02:56,510
♪♪

48
00:02:56,593 --> 00:02:59,012
-Is it a little weird for you?
-[Daniel] Yeah.

49
00:02:59,096 --> 00:03:01,515
[Sophia] <i>But... this is my story.</i>

50
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:06,895
So, this all started because
you never knew your dad.

51
00:03:06,978 --> 00:03:08,814
Yeah, yeah.

52
00:03:08,897 --> 00:03:10,691
[♪ soft music playing]

53
00:03:10,774 --> 00:03:15,570
<i>Growing up, was always</i>
<i>skeptical of my father.</i>

54
00:03:15,654 --> 00:03:18,990
Uh, he was Hispanic, of course,
with the last name Arguelles.

55
00:03:19,074 --> 00:03:22,035
And I would go up to him
and put my arm up against his

56
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:24,121
and I'd say, "Hey, how come
I'm white and you're brown?"

57
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:29,918
So, when I was 21, I did press my mother

58
00:03:30,502 --> 00:03:32,337
if Jim was my real father.

59
00:03:32,754 --> 00:03:34,297
[Sophia] What did she say?

60
00:03:34,381 --> 00:03:37,050
She confessed, "No, he wasn't."

61
00:03:37,134 --> 00:03:40,303
And I said, "Okay, who's my dad?"

62
00:03:40,887 --> 00:03:42,389
She said, "I really don't know."

63
00:03:43,849 --> 00:03:48,562
<i>And when I was, you know, 18, 19 years old</i>
<i>in Hollywood, everybody was telling me,</i>

64
00:03:48,645 --> 00:03:51,022
<i>"Wow, you look just like a young Elvis."</i>

65
00:03:51,106 --> 00:03:55,110
<i>I like to sing, I like to dance,</i>
<i>I like to act.</i>

66
00:03:55,569 --> 00:03:56,778
[imitating Elvis Presley]
How are you doing, everybody?

67
00:03:56,862 --> 00:03:59,239
That's right, it's me, Elvis Presley.

68
00:03:59,322 --> 00:04:01,867
You know, I'm doing my new album,
and it's called <i>Loving You.</i>

69
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:04,911
You had a lot of Elvis impressions,
impersonations around the house.

70
00:04:04,995 --> 00:04:06,872
[normal] Yeah, I walk around
like I'm Elvis, you know?

71
00:04:06,955 --> 00:04:07,998
I gotta be Elvis, right?

72
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:12,461
[Sophia] So, you joined Ancestry
to find your father.

73
00:04:12,544 --> 00:04:15,755
It wasn't until seven years later
you found a half-brother.

74
00:04:15,839 --> 00:04:17,549
[inhales] Right.

75
00:04:17,632 --> 00:04:21,887
He just said,
"Hey, uh, I'm Mike, and we matched."

76
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:24,347
And I was like, "Oh, brothers, wow.

77
00:04:24,431 --> 00:04:26,850
Okay, then you know
who your dad is, maybe?"

78
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:28,477
"Yeah, I know who he is."

79
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:32,272
And who's your dad?

80
00:04:33,023 --> 00:04:34,191
[Daniel] You know.

81
00:04:34,274 --> 00:04:35,692
Can you say it for me?

82
00:04:35,776 --> 00:04:36,985
Charles Manson.

83
00:04:40,614 --> 00:04:41,823
[Sophia sniffles]

84
00:04:44,743 --> 00:04:47,496
♪♪

85
00:05:01,009 --> 00:05:02,636
[♪ "My World" by Charles Manson playing]

86
00:05:02,719 --> 00:05:06,640
<i>♪ My world is a sad world ♪</i>

87
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:11,853
<i>♪ Often wonder if there's blame ♪</i>

88
00:05:12,813 --> 00:05:14,439
<i>♪ Such a fool ♪</i>

89
00:05:14,523 --> 00:05:17,526
<i>-♪ In a mad, mad world ♪</i>
-[Sophia] Hi, Puppa. [clicks tongue]

90
00:05:19,486 --> 00:05:21,321
Did you have a good day?

91
00:05:21,863 --> 00:05:23,573
<i>♪ Such a fool in a... ♪</i>

92
00:05:23,657 --> 00:05:27,369
<i>Charles Manson, to me,</i>
<i>he was just this cult leader</i>

93
00:05:27,452 --> 00:05:30,914
<i>and this murderer I had</i>
<i>read about on Wikipedia.</i>

94
00:05:31,832 --> 00:05:33,583
<i>When I found out</i>
<i>he was my grandfather,</i>

95
00:05:33,667 --> 00:05:35,669
<i>I didn't know what he looked like...</i>

96
00:05:37,337 --> 00:05:40,257
<i>so I decided to YouTube "Charles Manson."</i>

97
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:43,593
[newscaster 1]
<i>Breaking news, Charles Manson,</i>

98
00:05:43,677 --> 00:05:45,971
<i>cult leader</i>
<i>who led a string of murders</i>

99
00:05:46,054 --> 00:05:48,223
<i>in the 1960s is dead.</i>

100
00:05:48,306 --> 00:05:49,891
[newscaster 2]
<i>With a swastika on his forehead</i>

101
00:05:49,975 --> 00:05:51,601
<i>and eyes wild with violence,</i>

102
00:05:51,685 --> 00:05:54,145
<i>Charles Manson was the face of evil.</i>

103
00:05:54,229 --> 00:05:57,107
Maybe it was that look
in Charles Manson's eyes

104
00:05:57,190 --> 00:05:59,609
that convinced people
to murder in his name.

105
00:05:59,693 --> 00:06:03,530
[newscaster 3] <i>He was once called</i>
<i>"the most dangerous man in America."</i>

106
00:06:03,613 --> 00:06:06,825
Forty years later
and the fascination hasn't faded.

107
00:06:06,908 --> 00:06:09,202
People are still interested
in Charles Manson.

108
00:06:09,286 --> 00:06:11,788
Twenty years ago today,
America was stunned

109
00:06:11,872 --> 00:06:14,666
by one of the most brutal
killing sprees in history.

110
00:06:14,749 --> 00:06:16,626
[newscaster 4] <i>He's been called,</i>
<i>"Madman, mobster,</i>

111
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:18,086
<i>and monster, and murderer."</i>

112
00:06:18,169 --> 00:06:21,965
The center of a kind of
horrible fascination that will not die.

113
00:06:22,048 --> 00:06:24,551
He's a dangerous influence on society.

114
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:26,803
<i>Dangerous, highly dangerous.</i>

115
00:06:27,304 --> 00:06:28,972
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

116
00:06:29,055 --> 00:06:31,099
[newscaster 5] <i>In a scene</i>
<i>described by one investigator</i>

117
00:06:31,182 --> 00:06:33,435
<i>as reminiscent of a weird religious rite,</i>

118
00:06:33,518 --> 00:06:36,771
<i>five persons, including actress,</i>
<i>Sharon Tate, were found dead</i>

119
00:06:36,855 --> 00:06:38,481
<i>at the home of Miss Tate and her husband,</i>

120
00:06:38,565 --> 00:06:40,442
<i>screen director, Roman Polanski.</i>

121
00:06:40,525 --> 00:06:42,277
<i>Miss Tate was eight months pregnant</i>

122
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:46,239
<i>and was found with a rope around her neck</i>
<i>attached to the body of a man.</i>

123
00:06:46,323 --> 00:06:48,742
<i>Among the other victims</i>
<i>were Hollywood hairstylist,</i>

124
00:06:48,825 --> 00:06:51,995
<i>Jay Sebring,</i>
<i>and coffee heiress, Abigail Folger.</i>

125
00:06:52,078 --> 00:06:55,915
[Thomas Noguchi]
<i>Cause of death, multiple stab wounds</i>

126
00:06:55,999 --> 00:06:58,084
<i>of the chest and back.</i>

127
00:06:58,168 --> 00:07:00,837
[newscaster 5] <i>One officer summed up</i>
<i>the murders when he said,</i>

128
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:05,300
<i>"In all my years, I have never seen</i>
<i>anything like this before."</i>

129
00:07:05,383 --> 00:07:07,260
[newscaster 6]
<i>A short time later, not far away,</i>

130
00:07:07,344 --> 00:07:09,638
a middle-aged couple was found murdered

131
00:07:09,721 --> 00:07:11,306
in similar circumstances.

132
00:07:12,015 --> 00:07:14,142
[newscaster 7]
<i>Leno LaBianca, a supermarket owner,</i>

133
00:07:14,225 --> 00:07:18,688
<i>and his wife had both been</i>
<i>stabbed to death, repeated stab wounds.</i>

134
00:07:18,772 --> 00:07:22,692
<i>On his body, the word "war"</i>
<i>had been carved in the chest.</i>

135
00:07:22,776 --> 00:07:25,195
<i>Then, with blood, the killer had scrawled</i>

136
00:07:25,278 --> 00:07:27,656
<i>the words, "Death to Pigs."</i>

137
00:07:27,739 --> 00:07:29,407
[♪ intense music playing]

138
00:07:29,491 --> 00:07:31,785
[newscaster 6] <i>Five members</i>
<i>of a so-called religious cult,</i>

139
00:07:31,868 --> 00:07:36,039
<i>including Charles Manson,</i>
<i>the guru, or high priest,</i>

140
00:07:36,122 --> 00:07:39,459
have been indicted in the murder
of Sharon Tate and six others.

141
00:07:39,542 --> 00:07:42,003
[Vincent Bugliosi] <i>These defendants</i>
<i>literally entered the homes of citizens</i>

142
00:07:42,087 --> 00:07:44,089
<i>of this community at random</i>

143
00:07:44,172 --> 00:07:46,758
and literally slaughtered the victims
in an orgy of murder.

144
00:07:47,258 --> 00:07:48,343
[newscaster 8]
<i>All the elements are present</i>

145
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:52,180
<i>for one of the most sensational</i>
<i>murder trials in American history.</i>

146
00:07:52,263 --> 00:07:54,182
<i>The involvement of a mystical hippie clan,</i>

147
00:07:54,265 --> 00:07:57,227
<i>which despised</i>
<i>the straight affluent society.</i>

148
00:07:57,310 --> 00:08:00,939
<i>Young girls, supposedly under the spell</i>
<i>of a bearded Svengali,</i>

149
00:08:01,022 --> 00:08:03,274
<i>who allegedly masterminded</i>
<i>the seven murders.</i>

150
00:08:03,358 --> 00:08:05,944
[newscaster 7] <i>In a conspiracy,</i>
<i>the acts of one member</i>

151
00:08:06,027 --> 00:08:07,904
<i>are attributed to all members.</i>

152
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:11,074
<i>Under that approach, they needn't prove</i>
<i>that he actually did the killing.</i>

153
00:08:11,157 --> 00:08:12,951
It's all a play, isn't it?

154
00:08:13,034 --> 00:08:14,577
[newscaster 7] <i>The act</i>
<i>of the murders themselves</i>

155
00:08:14,661 --> 00:08:17,455
<i>will be the case against the other members</i>
<i>of the so-called Family.</i>

156
00:08:18,164 --> 00:08:21,209
<i>Susan Atkins, Patricia Krenwinkel,</i>

157
00:08:21,292 --> 00:08:24,838
<i>Leslie Van Houten, Charles Watson.</i>

158
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:26,756
[Vincent] <i>Manson's principle motive</i>

159
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:28,842
<i>for ordering the seven</i>
<i>Tate-LaBianca murders</i>

160
00:08:28,925 --> 00:08:31,386
<i>was his fanatical obsession</i>
<i>with </i>"Helter Skelter,"

161
00:08:31,886 --> 00:08:35,140
<i>a term he borrowed from the English</i>
<i>musical recording group, The Beatles.</i>

162
00:08:35,223 --> 00:08:38,643
To ignite Helter Skelter,
to start the Black/white, uh, revolution,

163
00:08:38,727 --> 00:08:41,229
Manson envisioned that white people

164
00:08:41,312 --> 00:08:43,022
would turn against the Black man
if they thought

165
00:08:43,106 --> 00:08:45,025
the Black man had committed
these seven murders.

166
00:08:45,108 --> 00:08:47,027
<i>The victims, by necessity, had to be white</i>

167
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:49,487
<i>and preferably in the white establishment.</i>

168
00:08:50,447 --> 00:08:52,032
[newscaster 1] <i>The jury</i>
<i>hearing the charges</i>

169
00:08:52,115 --> 00:08:54,409
<i>against Charles Manson</i>
<i>and three girl members</i>

170
00:08:54,492 --> 00:08:57,287
<i>of his so-called Family</i>
<i>brought in its verdict.</i>

171
00:08:57,370 --> 00:09:01,291
<i>All were found guilty</i>
<i>of murder in the first degree.</i>

172
00:09:01,374 --> 00:09:03,585
[newscaster 2]
<i>Manson then shouted at the jurors,</i>

173
00:09:03,668 --> 00:09:04,919
<i>"You're all guilty.</i>

174
00:09:05,003 --> 00:09:07,797
<i>We are still not allowed</i>
<i>to put on a defense."</i>

175
00:09:07,881 --> 00:09:10,800
The press has been the mouthpiece
of the prosecution.

176
00:09:10,884 --> 00:09:14,095
And they've succeeded in brainwashing
a lot of people.

177
00:09:14,179 --> 00:09:16,806
Charlie never preached a “Race War."

178
00:09:16,890 --> 00:09:19,309
-That's what the DA gave you for reality.
-[Tom Snyder] Okay.

179
00:09:19,392 --> 00:09:20,769
He stood in the courtroom and said,

180
00:09:20,852 --> 00:09:22,479
<i>"This man did this,</i>
<i>and this man did that,"</i>

181
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:23,563
<i>and you all believed him.</i>

182
00:09:23,646 --> 00:09:25,857
[♪ music fades out]

183
00:09:29,444 --> 00:09:31,071
[Sophia clears throat, sniffles]

184
00:09:31,613 --> 00:09:33,823
[Sophia] <i>Those first few days</i>
<i>after I found out...</i>

185
00:09:35,533 --> 00:09:38,787
<i>I don't think I've ever been</i>
<i>that low in my life.</i>

186
00:09:39,370 --> 00:09:40,997
<i>I kinda hated myself.</i>

187
00:09:43,249 --> 00:09:44,667
<i>I hated him.</i>

188
00:09:48,630 --> 00:09:49,839
[exhales]

189
00:09:51,716 --> 00:09:53,593
<i>I've always been the good one.</i>

190
00:09:54,302 --> 00:09:56,971
<i>I've always been the one, or the kid,</i>

191
00:09:57,055 --> 00:09:59,891
<i>with her head screwed on right.</i>

192
00:09:59,974 --> 00:10:03,353
<i>I would always get these, like,</i>
<i>integrity or caring awards, and...</i>

193
00:10:04,896 --> 00:10:08,691
<i>all of the teachers would tell my parents</i>
<i>how nice I was to everybody and...</i>

194
00:10:10,443 --> 00:10:12,529
if someone was lonely,
I'd go sit next to them,

195
00:10:12,612 --> 00:10:16,407
or if I saw someone bullied,
I'd stand up against the bully and...

196
00:10:18,159 --> 00:10:22,831
I always took really great pride
in being this moral, good person.

197
00:10:22,914 --> 00:10:25,125
[♪ pensive music playing]

198
00:10:25,208 --> 00:10:27,377
And then you find out
your grandfather's Manson...

199
00:10:27,460 --> 00:10:29,546
[sobbing]

200
00:10:32,215 --> 00:10:33,967
...the most hated man.

201
00:10:35,802 --> 00:10:39,681
How could my blood do something so...

202
00:10:40,473 --> 00:10:41,474
atrocious?

203
00:10:41,558 --> 00:10:43,393
How could my blood...

204
00:10:44,310 --> 00:10:46,646
be the cause of other people's bloodshed?

205
00:10:46,729 --> 00:10:48,565
[♪ tense music playing]

206
00:10:48,648 --> 00:10:50,525
<i>If it's true that there</i>
<i>was something in him</i>

207
00:10:50,608 --> 00:10:53,903
<i>that made him this satanic cult leader...</i>

208
00:10:55,446 --> 00:10:57,866
<i>do my dad and I</i>
<i>have that in our blood too?</i>

209
00:10:58,408 --> 00:10:59,534
[reel clicking]

210
00:10:59,617 --> 00:11:00,743
<i>I have his eyes.</i>

211
00:11:02,036 --> 00:11:03,872
<i>His hands are my dad's.</i>

212
00:11:05,415 --> 00:11:06,833
It fucks with you.

213
00:11:10,128 --> 00:11:13,548
<i>Was he born evil or was it something else?</i>

